    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A mountain kingdom centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lake-van">Lake Van</a>, called Biainili by its own kings, that rivalled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> in the 9th–7th centuries BCE.</li><li>Its kings built rock fortresses, storehouses and canals; Menua’s canal still carries water to the city of Van.</li><li>Weakened by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> and Cimmerians, Urartu vanished around 590 BCE, and its lands became the Armenian heartland.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Yerevan, capital of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#armenia">Armenia</a>, traces its origin to the Urartian fortress of Erebuni, founded by Argishti I in 782 BCE, and celebrated its 2,750th anniversary in 1968.</p></aside>
    </section>

