    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#shalmaneser-iii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shalmaneser-iii.jpg" alt="Shalmaneser III" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#shalmaneser-iii">Shalmaneser III</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shalmaneser_III_(relief_detail).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin FRCP(Glasg), CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Urartu, which its own kings called Biainili, took shape in the ninth century BCE in the highlands around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lake-van">Lake Van</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#anatolia">Anatolia</a>. Assyrian texts of the thirteenth century BCE already mention the lands of Uruatri and Nairi, a patchwork of mountain chiefdoms that Assyrian kings raided for horses, metal and captives, and this sustained pressure probably encouraged their unification. The first known king, Arame, appears in the annals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shalmaneser-iii">Shalmaneser III</a>, who sacked his royal city of Arzashkun in 856 BCE. A new dynasty began with Sarduri I, who c. 840 BCE built a citadel on the great rock of Van, the city of Tushpa, and left the earliest Urartian royal inscriptions, written in the Assyrian language. His son Ishpuini and grandson Menua began writing in Urartian, a language related to Hurrian, using a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cuneiform">cuneiform</a> script adapted from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#assyrian-empire">Assyria</a>, and extended royal power to the religious centre of Musasir in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#zagros-mountains">Zagros</a>, to the lands north of Lake Van and towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lake-urmia">Lake Urmia</a>. Under Argishti I and Sarduri II in the eighth century BCE Urartu reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mount-ararat">Ararat</a> plain, the basin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lake-sevan">Lake Sevan</a>, north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iran">Iran</a> and the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#euphrates">Euphrates</a>, becoming one of the largest states of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#near-east">Near East</a> and the principal rival of Assyria.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#sargon-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sargon-ii.jpg" alt="Sargon II" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#sargon-ii">Sargon II</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sargon_II,_Iraq_Museum_in_Baghdad.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin FRCP(Glasg), CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Urartu was a land of high plateaus and mountain valleys with long, harsh winters, and its prosperity depended on making the most of short growing seasons. The kings invested heavily in irrigation: Menua’s canal, which carries water for more than 50 kilometres from springs south of Van to the city, is still in use and was long known locally as the canal of Shamiram. Royal inscriptions record the planting of vineyards, orchards and fields alongside the founding of fortresses, and excavated storerooms at Karmir Blur, Ayanis and Bastam held rows of huge clay jars, some marked with their capacity, for wine, grain and oil. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#horse-breeding">Horse breeding</a> was renowned, and the Assyrian account of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sargon-ii">Sargon II</a>’s campaign of 714 BCE describes the region’s herds and the training of cavalry horses. Urartian metalworkers produced bronze shields, helmets, quivers, belts and large cauldrons decorated with lions, bulls and winged figures. Economic life centred on the king and the fortress centres, which collected and redistributed produce, while most of the population probably lived as farmers and pastoralists in villages. Religion was organised around a state pantheon headed by Haldi, with the storm god Teisheba and the sun god Shivini; the rock niche of Meher Kapısı near Van lists dozens of deities and the sacrifices due to each.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#tiglath-pileser-iii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tiglath-pileser-iii.jpg" alt="Tiglath-pileser III" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#tiglath-pileser-iii">Tiglath-pileser III</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tilglath_pileser_iii.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown artistUnknown artist, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Urartu’s military power rested on its fortresses. Its kings built citadels on rocky ridges throughout the kingdom, with massive walls of dressed stone set on rock-cut foundations, towers and buttresses, storerooms and cisterns. Tushpa, Erebuni, Argishtihinili, Teishebaini, Ayanis and many smaller strongholds formed a network that controlled valleys and routes and housed garrisons and supplies. Campaigns, recorded in royal annals carved on rock faces such as Argishti I’s inscription on the citadel of Van, brought back thousands of captives together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cattle">cattle</a>, sheep and horses. The army included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#chariot">chariots</a>, cavalry and infantry armed with spears, bows and round bronze shields. For more than a century Urartu contested northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#syria">Syria</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#taurus-mountains">Taurus</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#assyrian-empire">Assyria</a>. Its high point and its first great defeat came under Sarduri II, whose alliance with Arpad and other Syrian states was crushed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tiglath-pileser-iii">Tiglath-pileser III</a> in 743 BCE; in 735 BCE the Assyrian king reached Tushpa but could not take the citadel. In 714 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sargon-ii">Sargon II</a>, informed by spies that Rusa I had just been defeated by the Cimmerians, marched round <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lake-urmia">Lake Urmia</a>, routed Rusa’s army in the mountains, devastated the country and sacked Musasir, carrying off the treasures of the temple of Haldi.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Urartu was a centralised monarchy in which the king, styled ‘great king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#negus-nagast">king of kings</a>, king of Biainili, lord of Tushpa’, acted as the agent of the god Haldi; royal inscriptions present conquests and building works as achieved ‘by the greatness of Haldi’. The dynasty founded by Sarduri I ruled for most or all of the kingdom’s history, usually passing from father to son. The realm was divided into provinces under governors, and administration was built around fortress centres that served at once as palaces, storehouses, temples and garrison towns. The kings deliberately founded new centres and often populated them with deported captives: Argishti I’s foundation inscription at Erebuni, the origin of modern Yerevan, records that in 782 BCE he settled 6,600 prisoners from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hatti">Hatti</a> and Supani there. Writing served both royal display and administration, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cuneiform">cuneiform</a> on stone and clay and a little-understood native hieroglyphic script used mainly for marking containers. Clay bullae, seals and tablets from sites such as Bastam, Karmir Blur and Ayanis show officials tracking goods and personnel. The religious centre of Musasir, ruled by its own king but closely bound to the Urartian crown, was reportedly where Urartian kings were crowned before Haldi, so its sack by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sargon-ii">Sargon II</a> struck at royal legitimacy as well as the treasury.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#neo-assyrian-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/neo-assyrian-empire.jpg" alt="Neo-Assyrian Empire" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#neo-assyrian-empire">Neo-Assyrian Empire</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Assyria.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ningyou, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Urartu was the most serious and lasting rival the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#neo-assyrian-empire">Neo-Assyrian Empire</a> faced in the north, and Assyrian texts, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sargon-ii">Sargon II</a>’s long letter to the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#assur">Ashur</a> describing the campaign of 714 BCE, are a major source for its history. Its engineering left visible traces: Menua’s canal still waters Van, and fortress sites such as Van Kalesi, Çavuştepe, Ayanis and Erebuni are among the most impressive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iron-age">Iron Age</a> remains in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#anatolia">Anatolia</a> and the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caucasus">Caucasus</a>. Urartian columned halls and rock-cut tombs are often cited as forerunners of Median and Achaemenid architecture, and Urartian bronze and stone working influenced neighbouring peoples, although the origin of some bronze cauldron attachments found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#greece">Greece</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#italy">Italy</a> is still debated. The kingdom’s name survives in the biblical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mount-ararat">Ararat</a>, where in the Book of Genesis Noah’s ark came to rest; the modern Mount Ararat received that name much later. Urartu’s lands became the heartland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#armenians">Armenians</a>, whose language and identity took shape there after its fall, and Armenians regard Urartu as part of their ancient heritage. Yerevan dates its foundation to Argishti I’s fortress of Erebuni and celebrated its 2,750th anniversary in 1968. The rediscovery of Urartu by nineteenth- and twentieth-century scholars, notably Boris Piotrovsky at Karmir Blur, revealed a civilisation known before only from Assyrian accounts.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#rusa-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rusa-ii.jpg" alt="Rusa II" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#rusa-ii">Rusa II</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_cuneiform_inscription_of_Rusa_II.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Beko, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Urartu survived <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sargon-ii">Sargon II</a>’s invasion of 714 BCE, although Rusa I died soon afterwards; Assyrian sources claim he took his own life on hearing of the fall of Musasir. Under his successors Argishti II and especially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rusa-ii">Rusa II</a> in the first half of the seventh century BCE the kingdom recovered. Rusa II founded a series of new fortress centres, including Teishebaini at Karmir Blur near Yerevan, Bastam in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iran">Iran</a>, Ayanis on the eastern shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lake-van">Lake Van</a> and Rusahinili at Toprakkale near Tushpa, and relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> became largely peaceful. After the mid-seventh century the sources thin out: a King Sarduri sent gifts to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ashurbanipal">Ashurbanipal</a>, and later rulers are known only from scattered inscriptions with uncertain dates. Several fortresses, including Teishebaini, Ayanis and Bastam, were destroyed by fire, probably in the late seventh or early sixth century BCE, and arrowheads of Scythian type at Karmir Blur point to attacks by mounted nomads. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#babylonian-chronicles">Babylonian Chronicle</a> mentions a campaign to Urashtu in 608 BCE, and the Book of Jeremiah still names a kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mount-ararat">Ararat</a> in the early sixth century. The end is usually attributed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#medes">Medes</a> around 590 BCE, but its exact course is unknown, and some scholars place the collapse earlier. By the Achaemenid period the region was known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#armenia">Armenia</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Urartu united the highlands around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lake-van">Lake Van</a> into a fortress-based monarchy that rivalled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#neo-assyrian-empire">Neo-Assyrian Empire</a> for over a century, transformed a harsh mountain landscape with canals and storehouses, and passed its lands, and the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mount-ararat">Ararat</a>, to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#armenians">Armenians</a> and later Near Eastern history.</em></p>

